  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 233 PM MDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nNot a cloud in the sky today across our area with soundings and \ncurrent observations showing 40-50 degree dewpoint depressions and a \ndry vertical profile. Winds will remain light, with the exception of \nthe easternmost Colorado plains, where southerly winds at 10-15 mph \nwith sporadic gusts up to 25 mph will be possible. As such, elevated \nto locally critical fire weather conditions may occur over portions \nof Lincoln and Washington counties this afternoon although these \nconditions should be short-lived. High temperatures this afternoon \nare expected to be in the mid 70s for the low elevations, while the \nmountains experience a wider range of temperatures in the 50s and \n60s. Cloud cover from a decaying disturbance will drift over the \nregion tomorrow leading to temperatures a few degrees cooler than \ntoday, though still in the low 70s for the plains and Denver \nmetro.\n\nA return to southwest flow aloft and a weak shortwave will bring a \nslight chance (15-25% PoPs) for some rain and snow showers over the \nhigh terrain Monday, although no snow accumulation is expected and \nrainfall amounts will reach a trace at most. \n\nAn upper level trough will start to deepen off the Pacific \nNorthwest coast late Monday night. This upper level trough will \nslide south and become a strong cutoff low as it approaches the \nCalifornia coast and begins to move east on Wednesday. Southwest \nflow aloft will intensify across the Four Corners region ahead of\nthis cutoff low on Wednesday afternoon, bringing increasing \nmoisture and precipitation chances for the high country late \nWednesday into Thursday. \n\nUpper level flow will turn more southerly on Thursday, and \nthis is when the bulk of the Pacific moisture plume will arrive. \nHowever, as it stand now, our area will likely be under a \ndownsloping regime with south-southwesterly flow favoring orographic \nlift over southwestern Colorado and the San Juan Mountains \nprimarily. This will be a system to watch as the week progresses \ngiven that slight changes in the track of this cutoff low could \nbring the potential for heavy rain closer to our area. Regarding \nsnow chances with this system, the airmass appears to be too warm to \nsupport snow levels lower than 10,000-11,000ft until the next cold \nfront arrives on Saturday, likely after the moisture has moved \noff to the east.",
